void killJobs(List<Integer> ids) {
    for (int id : ids) {
        int index = jobIds.indexOf(id);

        if (index == -1) {
            System.err.println("kill: " + id + ": no such job");
            continue;
        }

        jobs.get(index).killPipeline();
    }
}

Now if your jobs are:
jobIds = [2, 5, 7]

then:
kill 7

correctly finds index 2.

10. Your Pipeline constructor is mostly good
I'd make it slightly cleaner:
public Pipeline(String input) {
    pipelineCommand = input.trim();

    String command = pipelineCommand;

    if (command.endsWith("&")) {
        isBackground = true;
        command = command.substring(0, command.length() - 1).trim();
    } else {
        isBackground = false;
    }

    pbs = new ArrayList<>();

    String[] commands = command.split("\\s*\\|\\s*");

    for (String subcommand : commands) {
        pbs.add(buildForkedShell(subcommand));
    }
}

This correctly handles:
ls | grep txt

and:
ls | grep txt &


11. Fix executePipeline()
Your approach here is basically the right one.
I'd write:
public List<Process> executePipeline() throws Exception {
    pbs.get(0).redirectInput(ProcessBuilder.Redirect.INHERIT);

    ProcessBuilder last = pbs.get(pbs.size() - 1);

    if (last.redirectOutput().equals(ProcessBuilder.Redirect.PIPE)) {
        last.redirectOutput(ProcessBuilder.Redirect.INHERIT);
    }

    processes = ProcessBuilder.startPipeline(pbs);

    if (!isBackground) {
        waitFor();
    }

    return processes;
}

This is consistent with the Java API: startPipeline() connects intermediate processes automatically, while the first input and final output are controlled by the first and last ProcessBuilder. OOracle Docs
Your buildForkedShell() already sets ordinary output to PIPE, which lets the final pipeline stage later be changed to INHERIT; if the final command has > file, its file redirect is preserved. Java's Redirect.PIPE is the default stream-based behavior, while INHERIT connects the subprocess to the shell's corresponding stream. OOracle Docs

